Project: MW–M31 Density Analysis Pipeline (Advanced Diagnostics, Animations & Publication Framework)
Hours Worked: 10.5

Work Summary:
Started with a Claude generated scaffold. 
Continued development of the MW–M31 merger density analysis pipeline by implementing advanced merger diagnostics, analytical halo profile fitting, temporal structural tracking, high-resolution visualization systems, synchronized animations, and automated publication-quality summary products. Expanded the framework into a comprehensive analysis environment capable of characterizing density evolution, progenitor interaction, halo structure, and merger morphology across the full simulation timeline.

Major accomplishments included:

• Developed Diagnostic 5 to investigate relative density dominance between the Milky Way and Andromeda progenitors throughout the merger. Implemented comparative radial density profile visualization together with local density-ratio diagnostics to identify regions of progenitor dominance, quantify merger interfaces, and monitor structural survival of each galactic core across representative evolutionary stages.

• Designed radial density ratio analysis using
      ρMW(r) / ρM31(r)
  to distinguish Milky Way-dominated, Andromeda-dominated, and equal-contribution regions. Added logarithmic profile visualization and synchronized ratio panels to improve interpretation of radial mass balance during coalescence.

• Implemented Diagnostic 6 introducing a spatial mass-mixing fraction metric
      f_mix(r,t)=min(ρMW,ρM31)/ρtotal
  to quantify local overlap between progenitor material. Constructed interpolated spatiotemporal heatmaps tracing outward propagation of the merger mixing front while computing time-averaged radial mixing profiles for long-term structural analysis.

• Developed interpolation framework for mapping logarithmically spaced radial density measurements onto uniform visualization grids, enabling smooth temporal evolution maps while preserving large dynamic range in halo structure.

• Implemented complete analytical profile fitting framework supporting Navarro–Frenk–White (NFW), Einasto, and Hernquist halo models. Developed reusable analytical density functions together with nonlinear least-squares optimization routines operating entirely in logarithmic density space to ensure balanced fitting across several orders of magnitude in density.

• Designed generalized fitting engine capable of automatically selecting valid fitting regions, estimating initial model parameters, performing constrained optimization, evaluating reduced χ² goodness-of-fit statistics, and handling convergence failures without interrupting pipeline execution.

• Automated extraction of physically meaningful structural parameters including NFW scale radius, Einasto characteristic radius and shape parameter α, Hernquist scale length, and corresponding model accuracy statistics for multiple snapshots sampled throughout the merger sequence.

• Constructed comparison figures overlaying measured density profiles with optimized analytical models across representative merger epochs, enabling direct visual assessment of model performance and structural evolution during galactic interaction.

• Implemented Diagnostic 8 to evaluate localized model accuracy through computation of normalized NFW residual fields
      Δ(r,t)=(ρmeasured−ρNFW)/ρNFW.
  Generated spatiotemporal residual heatmaps highlighting regions of excess concentration, post-merger cores, tidal stripping, and deviations from equilibrium halo structure throughout the simulation.

• Developed residual interpolation and averaging routines to characterize long-term systematic departures from analytical halo models while preserving radial dependence over time.

• Implemented Diagnostic 9 for structural telemetry tracking, monitoring chronological evolution of half-mass radii, fitted NFW scale radius, Einasto shape parameter, and reduced χ² statistics for all analytical models. Produced comparative evolution plots illustrating structural transformation and changing model performance throughout the merger.

• Designed synchronized multi-panel density profile animation engine displaying evolving spherical density profiles, progenitor-separated radial density distributions, and logarithmic density slope evolution simultaneously. Added ghost-history trajectories to preserve temporal context while emphasizing structural evolution frame-by-frame.

• Developed animated two-dimensional surface density visualization framework generating time-resolved projected morphology for the complete system together with separate Milky Way and Andromeda density maps. Implemented logarithmic smoothing, adaptive normalization, synchronized color scaling, and chronological annotations to improve interpretation of merger morphology.

• Built comprehensive publication-quality master summary dashboard integrating the principal density diagnostics into a unified multi-panel figure. Combined radial density evolution, projected density profiles, slope heatmaps, mass-mixing evolution, structural parameter tracking, analytical model performance, and representative morphology snapshots into a single presentation-ready overview.

• Standardized visualization styling throughout the density pipeline by implementing consistent dark-theme formatting, adaptive logarithmic scaling, synchronized axis normalization, automated legends, colorbar generation, interpolation utilities, figure labeling, and publication-quality export settings.

• Implemented automated filesystem cleanup, output manifest generation, storage accounting, execution summaries, and final reporting procedures documenting all generated figures, animations, and computational products produced during pipeline execution.

Overall Progress:
Substantially expanded the MW–M31 density analysis framework into a comprehensive structural evolution and visualization platform capable of performing analytical halo model fitting, merger mixing analysis, residual topology evaluation, parameter chronology, synchronized density animations, projected morphology visualization, and automated publication-ready figure generation. The pipeline now provides a complete end-to-end workflow for quantitative investigation of density evolution during galaxy mergers.
